    </div>
    <div class="tool-callout-action">
      <a href="/tools/gps-coordinates/" class="btn btn-primary">Test Live Coordinates →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Mocking Location in Google Chrome & Edge (DevTools Sensors)</h2>
  <p>
    Desktop browsers provide the fastest way to emulate custom coordinates without installing any software or mobile apps:
  </p>

  <div class="step-container">
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">1</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Open Developer Tools</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Press <code>F12</code> (or <code>Cmd + Option + I</code> on macOS) to open Chrome/Edge DevTools.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">2</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Open the Sensors Drawer</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Press <code>Cmd + Shift + P</code> (macOS) or <code>Ctrl + Shift + P</code> (Windows) &gt; Type <strong>"Sensors"</strong> &gt; Select <em>"Show Sensors"</em>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">3</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Select Preset or Custom Coordinates</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Under <strong>Location</strong>, choose from presets (e.g. London, Tokyo, San Francisco) or select <em>"Other..."</em> and enter custom Latitude and Longitude values.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>2. Mock Locations on Android (Developer Options)</h2>
  <p>
    Android includes a native <code>LocationManager</code> testing subsystem:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Enable Developer Options: Go to <strong>Settings &gt; About Phone</strong> &gt; Tap <strong>Build Number</strong> 7 times until you see <em>"You are now a developer!"</em>.</li>
    <li>Install a reputable mock GPS utility (e.g., GPS Emulator or Mock Locations).</li>
    <li>Go to <strong>Settings &gt; System &gt; Developer Options &gt; Select mock location app</strong> &gt; Choose your testing app.</li>
    <li>Set your desired coordinates and start the mock broadcast.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>3. Simulating GPX Routes on iOS with Xcode</h2>
  <p>
    Apple iOS does not have a direct user-facing toggle for mock coordinates, but developers can simulate locations using Apple's official development toolchain:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Connect your iPhone to a Mac running <strong>Xcode</strong>.</li>
    <li>Create a <code>.gpx</code> (GPS Exchange Format) file containing waypoint coordinates, elevation, and timestamps.</li>
    <li>In the Xcode Debug menu, select <strong>Simulate Location</strong> and choose your GPX waypoint file or route track.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. How Apps Detect Mock Locations Under the Hood</h2>
  <p>
    To prevent fraud in banking, gaming, and ride-hailing apps, mobile operating systems expose programmatic flags that inform applications when a mock location provider is active:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Android:</strong> <code>Location.isFromMockProvider()</code> (API 18+) or <code>Location.isMock()</code> (API 31+) returns <code>true</code> if the position was generated by a testing provider.</li>
    <li><strong>iOS:</strong> Comparison between cellular network timing and GPS timestamps identifies synthetic coordinates.</li>
  </ul>

